28
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:12,655
That led us to four questions. Where do delay

29
00:01:12,655 --> 00:01:15,410
minutes actually come from, and is that split

30
00:01:15,410 --> 00:01:18,165
stable over ten years? How much delay passes

31
00:01:18,165 --> 00:01:20,920
from one flight to the next? Do airports group

32
00:01:20,920 --> 00:01:23,675
into their own delay patterns? And can we turn

33
00:01:23,675 --> 00:01:26,430
all this into a tool a traveller can act on?

34
00:01:26,430 --> 00:01:29,185
These four questions map directly onto the

35
00:01:29,185 --> 00:01:31,940
rest of the talk.

36
00:01:32,390 --> 00:01:35,314
To answer these, we brought together five

37
00:01:35,314 --> 00:01:38,237
public datasets: flight-level records, the

38
00:01:38,237 --> 00:01:41,161
delay-cause cross-check, hourly weather from N

39
00:01:41,161 --> 00:01:44,085
O A A, airport details, and airline codes.

40
00:01:44,085 --> 00:01:47,009
Everything is public, so anyone can check our

41
00:01:47,009 --> 00:01:49,932
work. In total, that's over sixty-three

42
00:01:49,932 --> 00:01:52,856
million rows, and that scale is what makes our

43
00:01:52,856 --> 00:01:55,780
results believable.

44
00:01:56,230 --> 00:01:59,293
Our whole analysis runs as one pipeline with

45
00:01:59,293 --> 00:02:02,357
six stages: acquire, attribute, analyse the

46
00:02:02,357 --> 00:02:05,420
heavy tail, trace the cascade, model, and

47
00:02:05,420 --> 00:02:08,483
communicate. Each stage is a script, so one

48
00:02:08,483 --> 00:02:11,547
command regenerates every figure, and

49
00:02:11,547 --> 00:02:14,610
fifty-three tests pin every number back to its

50
00:02:14,610 --> 00:02:17,673
source. We built it this way so nothing is a

51
00:02:17,673 --> 00:02:20,737
black box, and anyone can reproduce our

52
00:02:20,737 --> 00:02:23,800
results.

53
00:02:24,250 --> 00:02:26,861
Our first finding answers where delay comes

54
00:02:26,861 --> 00:02:29,472
from. Seventy-three point three percent traces

55
00:02:29,472 --> 00:02:32,084
back to the airline itself, and only

56
00:02:32,084 --> 00:02:34,695
twenty-six point seven percent to the system

57
00:02:34,695 --> 00:02:37,306
around it. What surprised us is how stable

58
00:02:37,306 --> 00:02:39,917
this is: it barely moved across the whole

59
00:02:39,917 --> 00:02:42,529
